<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Overall, the skip doesn't have much context around it, there's no clear motives and no clear in-universe justification for existing. Which as a result, makes the skip feel close to a &quot;thing that does a thing&quot;, along with causing it to just feel like your average murder monster.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do agree with you that this SCP is basically just a thing that does a thing, but I don't fully agree with the &quot;murder monster&quot; part. It was my fault for not fully explaining it, but the cap doesn't always want to kill people. It just wants to hurt or humiliate its victim the best way it can without killing them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>there's no reason why the skip is a cap, or why the skip specifically has these anomalies (teleportation, telepathy, etc.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, I agree with the first part. It being a cap is kinda silly, but how it gets its anomalies was supposed to be a mystery, so it can make it interesting.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>or why it's interested in tricking people.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's not interested in tricking people. It's interested in other peoples' sorrow and will go to great lengths to torment them by making them do terrible stuff to themselves or others for something they will not get in the end. That's why it's called &quot;a very sadistic cap.&quot; It enjoys peoples' pain and misery, and that's its motivation to why it does these things.</p>
<p>(EDIT: wait no, I take back. You're right, I don't know why it's interested in tricking people. Maybe I put that in there because I wanted the SCP to be a good manipulator or something.)</p>
<p><strong>The Narrative</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>First off, this seems much closer to a single incident rather than a narrative. As there's little to no clear arc, and most questions the concept raised are still left unanswered.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, I wanted to make the story base on incidents that happened because of the SCP. This was just one of the incidents that the SCP caused.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation doesn't seem to be involved here at all, there isn't anything showing how they found it, what happened during its containment, and what they learned.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Foundation doesn't catch it because it teleports to different places around the world for another victim, but they are involved and they do learn it has a pattern of where it goes and when it strikes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The suicide note feels like a narrative device to drum up tension or make the skip seem more dangerous, which (to me at least) comes off as edgy and a bit cliched, considering how much this sort of storyline is seen in other media.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You're definitely right about this. I was trying to show how manipulative the SCP was and how it's not a thing to mess with, but the suicide note just made it edgy. I kinda cringed a bit when I read it again lol.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>on a side note, suicide in general can be a rather sensitive topic, if you do wish to include it, it will need to be handled carefully.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I didn't mean to do it in an insensitive way. I was trying to make the atmosphere creepy and mysterious at the same time by using the paintings, photos, and the suicide note.</p>
<p><strong>Hook</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This if fairly dull, as the skips effects or motives won't be able to carry the article alone, for this to work as a hook (IMO), they would need to be integrated and communicated effectively in the narrative (which currently they aren't). Generally speaking, you're looking to put the main idea or core narrative conflict in the hook.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I agree with this. I will try to change it soon.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, this is some really good critique. Thanks!</p>

