			<div class="content" id="post-content-4856141">
									

<p>Heyo!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>(any idea how to hide the text? For reason, my brain with two brain-cells can't seem to find the button for it…)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I usually go to this site an copy paste the syntax:</p>
<p><a href="https://www.wikidot.com/doc-wiki-syntax:collapsible-blocks">https://www.wikidot.com/doc-wiki-syntax:collapsible-blocks</a></p>
<p>sorry about all the edits, collapsible texts apparently don't like being formatted:(</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Well, I wanted to make the story base on incidents that happened because of the SCP. This was just one of the incidents that the SCP caused.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Incident logs alone won't be able to fully carry the story, as they don't show much of an arc, much less a strong payoff. When pitching the narrative, you're looking to show a brief summary of the whole story, from the setup to the payoff and the main conflict.- its essentially a <em>very</em> brief draft.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation doesn't catch it because it teleports to different places around the world for another victim, but they are involved and they do learn it has a pattern of where it goes and when it strikes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sounds a bit off to me, if it has a pattern then the Foundation should (theoretically) be able to contain it, or at least put more permanent and/or streamlined techniques in place to contain it. Currently their containment method feels fairly makeshift and rough.</p>
<p>This also wasn't mentioned in the narrative pitch much, which makes it hard for me to get an idea of the draft would look like.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I didn't mean to do it in an insensitive way. I wanted to make it creepy and mysterious at the same time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you're looking to add mystery, try to allow the story to slowly build anticipation on the questions the concept raised, rather than trying to shoehorn something in quickly.</p>
<p>Hope this cleared some stuff up, let me know if you want any additional feedback :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

